  "configuration_id": "config_003",
  "reason": "Evidence: config_002 (rna_only, multiprofile, pca_n2000, avgpool) achieved 0.591 acc, a substantial +10.6% gain over config_001 (singleprofile, 0.485), confirming that multiprofile representation is highly beneficial. However, we have only tested one aggregation method (avgpool) and do not know whether a more expressive transformer aggregation can further improve profile combination. Question: Does transformer aggregation outperform simple average pooling for combining multiple RNA profiles, holding encoder and modality fixed? This is a clean single-variable comparison against config_002. Why this is the best next experiment: It directly extends the current best configuration by changing only the aggregation mechanism, isolating that effect. The answer informs whether we should invest in transformer-based aggregation for all subsequent multimodal experiments (where aggregation becomes even more critical for combining profiles across modalities), or whether avgpool is sufficient and we should instead focus budget on RNA encoder upgrades (scvi, scgpt) or multimodal fusion. Under a limited budget, resolving this foundational design choice before adding further complexity (new encoders, multimodal fusion) is the most information-efficient use of the next experiment."
}

